<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>CFBundleDevelopmentRegion</key>
	<string>$(DEVELOPMENT_LANGUAGE)</string>
	<key>CFBundleDisplayName</key>
	<string>Aura</string>
	<key>CFBundleExecutable</key>
	<string>$(EXECUTABLE_NAME)</string>
	<key>CFBundleIdentifier</key>
	<string>$(PRODUCT_BUNDLE_IDENTIFIER)</string>
	<key>CFBundleInfoDictionaryVersion</key>
	<string>6.0</string>
	<key>CFBundleName</key>
	<string>aura</string>
	<key>CFBundlePackageType</key>
	<string>APPL</string>
	<key>CFBundleShortVersionString</key>
	<string>$(FLUTTER_BUILD_NAME)</string>
	<key>CFBundleSignature</key>
	<string>????</string>
	<key>CFBundleVersion</key>
	<string>$(FLUTTER_BUILD_NUMBER)</string>
	<key>LSRequiresIPhoneOS</key>
	<true/>
	<key>UILaunchStoryboardName</key>
	<string>LaunchScreen</string>
	<key>UIMainStoryboardFile</key>
	<string>Main</string>
	<key>UISupportedInterfaceOrientations</key>
	<array>
		<string>UIInterfaceOrientationPortrait</string>
		<string>UIInterfaceOrientationLandscapeLeft</string>
		<string>UIInterfaceOrientationLandscapeRight</string>
	</array>
	<key>UISupportedInterfaceOrientations~ipad</key>
	<array>
		<string>UIInterfaceOrientationPortrait</string>
		<string>UIInterfaceOrientationPortraitUpsideDown</string>
		<string>UIInterfaceOrientationLandscapeLeft</string>
		<string>UIInterfaceOrientationLandscapeRight</string>
	</array>
	<key>CADisableMinimumFrameDurationOnPhone</key>
	<true/>
	<key>UIApplicationSupportsIndirectInputEvents</key>
	<true/>

	<!--
	  Privacy-sensitive APIs the app actually uses. Each NS*UsageDescription
	  is required by App Store review; a missing entry triggers automatic
	  rejection (guideline 5.1.1).

	  Camera: image_picker (avatar / attachment capture), flutter_webrtc
	    (video calls when Pro plan enables realtime).
	  Microphone: record (correspondence audio recording),
	    flutter_webrtc (voice/video calls).
	  Photo library read: image_picker for attachment picking.
	  Photo library add: when the user saves attachment media back to
	    their library (institutional posts, announcements). Apple
	    treats add-only as a distinct authorization.

	  No tracking, no IDFA: NSUserTrackingUsageDescription intentionally
	    omitted. Aura does not use AppTrackingTransparency-gated APIs.
	    If a future SDK pulls in IDFA usage, add the key + prompt then.
	-->
	<key>NSCameraUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Aura uses the camera so you can record video for calls and capture photos for posts, attachments, and your profile.</string>
	<key>NSMicrophoneUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Aura uses the microphone so you can record voice messages and join voice or video calls.</string>
	<key>NSPhotoLibraryUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Aura uses your photo library so you can attach images to posts, messages, and announcements.</string>
	<key>NSPhotoLibraryAddUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Aura asks for permission to save images and downloads to your photo library when you choose to keep them.</string>

	<!--
	  Location: the Aura app itself does not request, store, or share
	  the user's location. Nothing under `lib/` references CoreLocation,
	  geolocator, or `Permission.location` — the only permissions Aura
	  asks for through `permission_handler` are camera and microphone.

	  These purpose strings exist because Apple's static analyzer
	  (`Transporter` / App Store Connect validator, error ITMS-90683)
	  flags the BINARY as referencing CoreLocation through linked
	  dependencies, and says so explicitly: "While your app might not use
	  these APIs, a purpose string is still required." Two dependencies
	  can carry that reference — the Firebase iOS SDK, and
	  `permission_handler`, whose iOS pod compiles every permission
	  handler it ships, location included, unless it is told not to.
	  Which of the two triggered it has not been established, and the
	  requirement is the same either way.

	  BOTH keys are needed. Build 26 delivered successfully on
	  2026-08-29 and Apple still returned ITMS-90683, naming
	  NSLocationAlwaysAndWhenInUseUsageDescription: the WhenInUse key
	  alone did not cover the reference. They are worded identically and
	  truthfully, so a reviewer can reconcile them with an app that has
	  no location feature.

	  THE DURABLE FIX IS TO STOP SHIPPING THE REFERENCE, not to describe
	  it better: `permission_handler` supports compile-time exclusion via
	  `GCC_PREPROCESSOR_DEFINITIONS` in the Podfile (`PERMISSION_LOCATION=0`,
	  `PERMISSION_LOCATION_ALWAYS=0`, keeping only CAMERA and MICROPHONE).
	  That is deliberately NOT done here: this repo commits no Podfile —
	  Flutter generates it during the build — so introducing one is a
	  change to the iOS build itself, and it does not belong in the middle
	  of a release that has only just built end to end. It is the right
	  next change, made on its own.
	-->
	<key>NSLocationWhenInUseUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Aura does not currently use your location. This permission is requested only if you later enable a feature that needs it (for example, surfacing nearby live sessions). Your location is never shared.</string>
	<key>NSLocationAlwaysAndWhenInUseUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Aura does not currently use your location. This permission is requested only if you later enable a feature that needs it (for example, surfacing nearby live sessions). Your location is never shared.</string>

	<!--
	  Background modes. We claim ONLY the modes the app actually uses:
	    - remote-notification: Firebase Messaging delivers push payloads
	      while the app is suspended (incoming-call ring, message alerts).
	  Each entry must be justifiable to App Store review — keep this list
	  minimal. Adding `audio`, `fetch`, or `voip` here without a corresponding
	  feature path (e.g. voip requires PushKit + CallKit) is a rejection trigger.
	-->
	<key>UIBackgroundModes</key>
	<array>
		<string>remote-notification</string>
		<!--
		  A/V reconstruction, iOS platform analysis (section 4). Without
		  `audio` an iOS call goes silent the moment the app leaves the
		  foreground — the person locks their screen mid-call and the other
		  side simply stops hearing them, with nothing said. This is declared
		  now because it is a build-time capability the implementation
		  requires; it remains UNCERTIFIED until a real device or TestFlight
		  run exercises it. See docs/av/AV_PRODUCT_CONTRACT.md.
		-->
		<string>audio</string>
		<!--
		  NOW EARNED, AND ONLY NOW. The comment above this array used to warn
		  that claiming `voip` without PushKit + CallKit is a rejection
		  trigger, which is exactly why it was absent: an FCM alert dressed as
		  a call is not a call, and declaring the capability for it would have
		  been a false claim about the binary.

		  AppDelegate.swift now registers a PKPushRegistry for `.voIP` and
		  reports every VoIP push to a CXProvider unconditionally — the two
		  halves Apple requires, present together. The mode is declared
		  because the app genuinely does this, not because we want the
		  delivery guarantees that come with it.
		-->
		<string>voip</string>
	</array>

	<!--
	  Custom URL scheme so external links of the form aura://… can wake
	  the app and route through `app_links` / notification-tap handling.
	  HTTPS Universal Links also work via Associated Domains (declared
	  in Runner.entitlements + apple-app-site-association at the
	  backend domain root).
	-->
	<key>CFBundleURLTypes</key>
	<array>
		<dict>
			<key>CFBundleURLName</key>
			<string>org.auraplatform.app</string>
			<key>CFBundleURLSchemes</key>
			<array>
				<string>aura</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
	</array>

	<!--
	  Encryption export compliance: app uses only standard HTTPS / WebRTC
	  encryption. Marks the binary as exempt from US export documentation
	  on every TestFlight upload (Apple defaults to "Yes" otherwise and
	  blocks builds until answered).
	-->
	<key>ITSAppUsesNonExemptEncryption</key>
	<false/>
</dict>
</plist>
